From 9110d8e62ce8112ffa644ce89c9e8249ce2eee80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mohammad Nauman <md.nauman@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 12:23:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] elanpress: Add driver for the Elan 04f3:0c3d press sensor

04f3:0c3d is a 64x88 px (~500 dpi, ~3.2x4.4 mm) press sensor sold as a
"Windows Hello" USB dongle (TEC TE-FPA2 and others). It has been in the
elan driver's id table, but that driver treats every device as a swipe
sensor: it crops each frame to 50 rows, films ~30 frames of a resting
finger and stitches them into a strip that differs on every press, and
hands the result to NBIS. bozorth3 returns a constant zero for any print
with fewer than 10 minutiae, and a patch this small yields 1-5, so
verification could never succeed on this device.

This driver speaks the same bulk protocol as the elan driver but treats
the pad as what it is:

- full 64x88 frames, press mode, up to three distinct frames kept per
  touch (the finger settles and rolls a little during a press);
- no device-side calibration loop (it deadlocks on this firmware); the
  background frame is validated against the device's own calibration
  mean instead, which also detects a finger that has not lifted yet;
- match-on-host by zero-mean normalised cross-correlation of locally
  normalised ridge images, searched over translation and +/-20 degrees of
  rotation, coarse-to-fine, spread over the CPUs; templates are the
  enrolled images (FPI_PRINT_RAW), ~5.6 KB each, 20 touches per
  enrolment;
- finger status reported once per touch rather than per frame.

Threshold 0.76 was derived from a 140-image pool on one pad (index 20
touches, middle/ring/thumb 12 each): it sits above every impostor score
(0/91 accepted) and passes ~60% of genuine touches with a 20-touch
enrolment. Coverage, not the matcher, is the limit on a sensor this
small; the enrolling UI should ask the user to shift the finger between
presses.

Trade-off stated up front: a template is the enrolled images themselves,
not minutiae, and can be inverted into a picture of the finger. That is
what the vendor's Windows driver stores as well, and it is the only kind
of template a 3x4 mm patch supports.

Derived from Filip Spanne's elanpress driver for 04f3:0c6e; that device
is not enabled here because it was not tested with these parameters.
